WEBVTT

1
00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:18.411
The crackling from the fireplace gave the impression that all was well in the March home.

2
00:00:18.412 --> 00:00:24.624
Another crackling sound, that of Jo pushing down on her knuckles, told a different story.

3
00:00:24.625 --> 00:00:28.546
"I wish this bothersome snow would stop."

4
00:00:28.547 --> 00:00:35.891
Jo banged the window as she watched the gigantic snowflakes silently piling up on the ground.

5
00:00:35.892 --> 00:00:40.971
"Jo," shrieked Amy, "that's so unladylike!"

6
00:00:40.972 --> 00:00:44.763
Amy smoothed her dress and caressed her curls.

7
00:00:44.764 --> 00:00:49.186
She could not stand it when her older sister acted like a boy.

8
00:00:49.187 --> 00:00:53.872
Jo, on the other hand, wished she could be a boy.

9
00:00:53.873 --> 00:00:58.571
She often complained: "Why do I have to wear this confining dress?

10
00:00:58.572 --> 00:01:04.694
Why can't I wear trousers and kick my legs up and gallop around freely, like a horse?"

11
00:01:04.695 --> 00:01:11.719
Whenever Jo said such things, her older sister Meg would gently shake her head and say:

12
00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:18.066
"Because whether you like it or not, you are a girl. And girls should behave as such."

13
00:01:18.067 --> 00:01:23.595
On this cold winter day, however, Meg did not scold her tomboy sister.

14
00:01:23.596 --> 00:01:28.680
In fact, she agreed with Jo about the endlessly falling snow.

15
00:01:28.681 --> 00:01:32.680
"It's so cold and miserable," Meg lamented.

16
00:01:32.681 --> 00:01:37.265
"When we were rich, we could warm up every room in the house."

17
00:01:37.266 --> 00:01:42.043
Now the only room that was heated was the living room in which they sat.

18
00:01:42.044 --> 00:01:46.066
"We didn't have to wear these unbecoming sweaters."

19
00:01:46.067 --> 00:01:51.034
Amy turned up her nose as if her sweater had a funny smell.

20
00:01:51.035 --> 00:01:57.728
"Now our concerns are about how Father is faring under these treacherous conditions," added Jo.

21
00:01:57.729 --> 00:02:03.414
She stomped over to the fireplace and rubbed her large hands together vigorously.

22
00:02:03.415 --> 00:02:10.778
Being over the age at which men could enlist in the army, Father had volunteered his services as a clergyman.

23
00:02:10.779 --> 00:02:16.511
The girls were too young to understand or to care what the Civil War was about.

24
00:02:16.512 --> 00:02:20.579
They only wanted it to end so Father could return home.

25
00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:24.489
At one time, the March family had been very wealthy.

26
00:02:24.490 --> 00:02:31.547
Meg and Jo, the two oldest girls, could remember a time when they received many gifts all year around.

27
00:02:31.548 --> 00:02:36.799
Now they didn't have enough money to buy even Christmas gifts for each other.

28
00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:40.441
"If you had five dollars . . ." Jo began.

29
00:02:40.442 --> 00:02:44.806
(At this period of time, five dollars was a lot of money.)

30
00:02:44.807 --> 00:02:50.169
"If you had five dollars," Jo repeated, "what would you buy?"

31
00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:55.218
The girls fell silent as their eyes grew bigger with fanciful thoughts.

32
00:02:55.219 --> 00:02:59.190
"I know what I would buy," Amy gushed at last.

33
00:02:59.191 --> 00:03:01.288
Her sisters turned to her.

34
00:03:01.289 --> 00:03:04.245
"Go on," urged Jo.

35
00:03:04.246 --> 00:03:09.598
Amy's cheeks got pink and she pressed her hands together excitedly.

36
00:03:09.599 --> 00:03:13.632
"I would buy a new set of watercolors," she said.

37
00:03:13.633 --> 00:03:21.194
"And then I would buy two new hairpins: one red with silver butterflies, like my best friend Kitty has.

38
00:03:21.195 --> 00:03:25.100
And the other blue like my eyes."

39
00:03:25.101 --> 00:03:31.530
"As for me," Meg said, "I would buy beautiful silk material for a new dress.

40
00:03:31.531 --> 00:03:37.446
Maybe I would have some money left over for gloves since I desperately need new ones."

41
00:03:37.447 --> 00:03:42.674
Meg held up her lovely hands, which were now pinkish red from the cold.

42
00:03:42.675 --> 00:03:48.525
Meg had many vanities but she took special pride in her hands.

43
00:03:48.526 --> 00:03:52.531
"I," Jo blurted in a loud, comical voice,

44
00:03:52.532 --> 00:04:03.139
"would buy four bottles of black ink, a case of pens, twenty rolls of paper, and three hundred . . ." she paused for dramatic emphasis.

45
00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:10.401
"Three hundred," she repeated again, "leather cases for all the books I am going to publish."

46
00:04:10.402 --> 00:04:19.560
Jo dreamed of being a writer. Nothing suited her more than to sit at her desk and enter a world of make-believe and fantasy.

47
00:04:19.561 --> 00:04:27.464
"You can't afford that many leather cases with only five dollars. That's ridiculous," Amy pointed out.

48
00:04:27.465 --> 00:04:34.517
"Well, so is this game," Jo retorted, "because none of us actually has five dollars!"

49
00:04:34.518 --> 00:04:38.090
"Girls," Meg said in her "motherly" voice.

50
00:04:38.091 --> 00:04:42.380
"Do stop arguing. You're behaving like children.

51
00:04:42.381 --> 00:04:47.943
Now, Beth, let's hear what you would do if you had five dollars."

52
00:04:47.944 --> 00:04:52.766
Beth had a faraway look in her eyes. She was smiling.

53
00:04:52.767 --> 00:04:57.775
"First of all, I'd buy a new pair of slippers for Mother.

54
00:04:57.776 --> 00:05:04.719
I noticed her old ones have several small holes in them," Beth said in her gentle, quiet way.

55
00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:08.879
The other girls glanced at the familiar slippers next to the sofa.

56
00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:15.578
They all felt rather guilty when they saw that the well-worn slippers did, indeed, have many holes.

57
00:05:15.579 --> 00:05:22.293
"And then I would buy Father a new scarf and hat in his favorite color," Beth continued.

58
00:05:22.294 --> 00:05:31.131
"If I had money left over, I would get each of you girls a handkerchief with your name on it. And one for Hannah, too."

59
00:05:31.132 --> 00:05:36.436
Of the four girls, Beth was by far the kindest and most loving.

60
00:05:36.437 --> 00:05:41.037
She never said a bad word about anyone nor did she ever complain.

61
00:05:41.038 --> 00:05:44.955
She had a positive view of everything and everyone.

62
00:05:44.956 --> 00:05:52.369
"Oh, Beth!" Jo ran over and wrapped her arms around her sister. "You make me feel so ashamed!

63
00:05:52.370 --> 00:06:04.016
Here I am thinking of myself, but you—" she sniffed, for Jo was a very emotional girl, "—you even thought of Hannah, our housekeeper."

64
00:06:04.017 --> 00:06:12.551
Meg and Amy also gave Beth a hug. They, too, were feeling a little embarrassed about their selfishness.

65
00:06:12.552 --> 00:06:19.240
Even though the sisters had different personalities, they were very close and loved one another very much.

66
00:06:19.241 --> 00:06:21.896
"Girls, what is going on?"

67
00:06:21.897 --> 00:06:25.345
The girls had not heard Mother come home.

68
00:06:25.346 --> 00:06:29.388
"Mother!" they cried and ran to give her a hug.

69
00:06:29.389 --> 00:06:36.811
Mother's hands and cheeks were cold from the air outside, but her hugs and kisses warmed the girls up instantly.

70
00:06:36.812 --> 00:06:43.140
Meg quickly removed Mother's heavy coat. Jo ran to get more wood for the fire.

71
00:06:43.141 --> 00:06:48.659
Amy grabbed Mother's sweater, which was folded up on her chair, and gave it to her.

72
00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:52.489
Beth quietly brought Mother's slippers to warm her feet.

73
00:06:52.490 --> 00:06:59.778
As Mother sat on her chair and rubbed her hands in front of the fire, Jo told her what they'd been talking about.

74
00:06:59.779 --> 00:07:03.398
"We were so selfish, Mother," admitted Jo.

75
00:07:03.399 --> 00:07:07.657
"Except for Beth. She only cares about other people."

76
00:07:07.658 --> 00:07:14.439
Mother smiled affectionately at each daughter and said: "I'm glad that you discovered your fault, girls.

77
00:07:14.440 --> 00:07:18.444
But finding out our faults is only the first step.

78
00:07:18.445 --> 00:07:22.776
What we do with this knowledge is more important."

79
00:07:22.777 --> 00:07:31.891
That evening, after a simple dinner, Mother and Hannah took inventory of the food pantry while the girls sat around the fireplace talking again.

80
00:07:31.892 --> 00:07:35.090
"I have some money saved up," Jo began.

81
00:07:35.091 --> 00:07:43.709
"It's only a little bit . . . but, well, I wanted . . . Mother . . . a little gift . . .," she stammered.

82
00:07:43.710 --> 00:07:52.689
"Oh, Jo! What a lovely thought!" Beth understood right away what her sister was trying hard to express.

83
00:07:52.690 --> 00:07:56.728
"What's a lovely thought?" Amy asked irritably.

84
00:07:56.729 --> 00:08:00.910
Unlike Beth, she had no idea what Jo was trying to say.

85
00:08:00.911 --> 00:08:05.594
"This Christmas none of us has much to give," Beth explained.

86
00:08:05.595 --> 00:08:11.434
"But with what we do have, Jo suggests that we should all give something to Mother."

87
00:08:11.435 --> 00:08:15.660
Beth smiled at Jo, who nodded enthusiastically.

88
00:08:15.661 --> 00:08:20.203
"That's exactly what I wanted to say, Beth," she said gratefully.

89
00:08:20.204 --> 00:08:23.984
Meg and Amy both agreed it was a splendid idea.

90
00:08:23.985 --> 00:08:29.452
They spent the rest of the evening discussing what Mother needed and what they could give her.

91
00:08:29.453 --> 00:08:38.654
Although the girls would not receive gifts themselves, they were quite happy to learn the true meaning of giving.
